CALL newPutForth(521, 1, 'There are excellent television shows worth watching — not as a guilty pleasure, but as genuine art. The prestige TV era produced work that rivals the best literature and film. Don\'t let the medium\'s history of mediocrity sell the current output short.');
CALL newPutForth(522, 1, 'The Investigation is methodical, quiet, and devastating. It reconstructs a murder case without ever showing the crime — just the work of prosecutors building a case. If you have patience for slow, serious drama, this is worth your time.');
CALL newPutForth(523, 1, 'Beartown is about a hockey town, a rape, and the way communities protect their own at the cost of their most vulnerable. It\'s beautifully made and hard to watch. Worth it.');
CALL newPutForth(524, 1, 'How To with John Wilson is unlike anything else on television. Part documentary, part diary, part accidental philosophy — it finds profound observations in the most mundane corners of New York City. Genuinely original.');
CALL newPutForth(525, 1, 'Chernobyl is the best limited series made in the prestige TV era. It\'s historically grounded, visually stunning, and morally serious. The final episode, where the actual witnesses testify, is as good as television gets.');
CALL newPutForth(526, 1, 'Succession is as good as its reputation suggests. The writing is razor-sharp, the performances are extraordinary, and the show understands power — how it\'s wielded, how it corrupts, and what it costs — better than almost anything else made.');
CALL newPutForth(622, 1, 'WandaVision was a genuine creative risk for the MCU — a limited series built around grief, identity, and decades of TV sitcom history. For most of its run, it delivered on that ambition in ways few expected from a Marvel property.');
CALL newPutForth(624, 1, 'The MCU works when singular creative voices drive it and fail when it\'s made by committee. The moment a project starts optimizing for franchise continuity over storytelling, you can feel it — and so can the audience.');
CALL newPutForth(625, 1, 'WandaVision was wise to resist the fan-theory bait. Last-minute cameos or Mephisto reveals would have collapsed the story into a fanservice delivery mechanism. The decision to stay true to Wanda\'s grief was the right call.');
CALL newPutForth(626, 1, 'Polish and standards get results. The difference between work that lasts and work that doesn\'t is usually craft — the willingness to do it again until it\'s right. That applies to TV, film, music, and everything else.');
CALL newPutForth(627, 1, 'WandaVision\'s major plot beats were widely predicted before the finale aired. That\'s the inevitable cost of corporate IP storytelling — enough is known about the characters that surprises are rare. It doesn\'t ruin the show, but it does limit it.');
CALL newPutForth(679, 1, 'WandaVision was brave for six episodes and conventional for one. The finale snapped back to a standard MCU action climax — heroes fight villain, powers clash, day is saved. After building something genuinely different, the ending was a retreat.');
CALL newPutForth(927, 1, 'American culture — music, film, literature, television — has been the dominant creative force in the world for the past century. That\'s not jingoism; it\'s a historical observation. And it\'s significantly because America absorbs and amplifies cultural contributions from everywhere.');
CALL newPutForth(946, 1, 'American culture is enriched by immigration. Jazz, hip hop, rock and roll, gospel, blues — none of these would exist in their American form without the contributions of immigrant and diaspora communities. That\'s the story of American culture.');
CALL newPutForth(947, 1, 'Black American culture built the soundtrack of the 20th century. Rock, hip hop, jazz, blues, soul, R&B — the influence is foundational, and the acknowledgment of it in mainstream culture is still catching up to the reality.');
CALL newPutForth(948, 1, 'Elvis took sounds, styles, and in some cases specific recordings from Black artists who couldn\'t reach the mainstream audiences he could. That\'s a documented pattern, not a controversial claim. He was talented. He also habitually ripped people off.');
CALL newPutForth(949, 1, 'Elvis was commercially dominant and culturally significant, but calling him the king of rock and roll erases the artists he learned from and built upon. The crown is debatable at minimum.');
CALL newPutForth(953, 1, 'Black artists have historically been filtered, diluted, and blocked from the mainstream by industry gatekeepers who preferred white artists performing similar material. That\'s the documented history of American popular music.');
CALL newPutForth(1013, 1, 'Seattle has produced Nirvana, Pearl Jam, Soundgarden, Jimi Hendrix. The grunge era alone would put Seattle in the top tier of American music cities. The city\'s output is disproportionate to its size.');
CALL newPutForth(1014, 1, 'Seattle is the best music city in the world. Nirvana, Hendrix, Pearl Jam, Soundgarden — no other city of comparable size has had this level of impact on the trajectory of popular music. The Pacific Northwest produced something extraordinary.');
CALL newPutForth(1015, 1, 'Nirvana is the best band in history. The combination of Kurt Cobain\'s songwriting, the raw force of their performances, and the way Nevermind cracked the mainstream open — no band had more impact on the music that followed.');
CALL newPutForth(1016, 1, 'Nevermind is the best album of the 1990s. Every track holds up. The production is perfect for the material. And the album didn\'t just capture the decade — it helped define it. Nothing else from the 90s has aged as well.');
CALL newPutForth(1019, 1, 'Blink-182 became the defining band of pop punk — catchy, juvenile, and enormously successful. They\'re a genuine cultural artifact of late-90s suburban teenage experience. Whether that\'s praise or criticism depends on your perspective.');
CALL newPutForth(1021, 1, 'Making punk palatable to pop audiences strips it of the confrontation and rage that make it meaningful. You can make it energetic and melodic — that\'s fine. But punk that doesn\'t disturb anyone isn\'t really punk.');
CALL newPutForth(1022, 1, 'Suburban middle-class angst is real, if not the most urgent angst in the world. The emotional experience of being seventeen and bored and restless doesn\'t require material poverty to be genuine. Pop punk is an authentic expression of that experience.');
CALL newPutForth(1023, 1, 'The Chronic changed rap. Dr. Dre\'s production established the G-funk sound and proved that West Coast hip hop could define the mainstream. It\'s one of the most important albums in popular music history.');
CALL newPutForth(1024, 1, 'Hip hop going mainstream in the 1990s was the most important musical development of that decade. It wasn\'t just a genre crossing over — it was Black American art claiming its rightful place as the dominant force in popular culture.');
CALL newPutForth(1026, 1, 'The album of a decade should soundtrack its most defining moments — the ones that will make sense to anyone who lived through it. Technical achievement matters less than whether the music captures what those years felt like.');
CALL newPutForth(1029, 1, 'Gangsta\'s Paradise is one of the great rap songs — and the album around it is underrated. Coolio\'s ability to blend menace and reflection gave the record more depth than its single-hit reputation suggests.');
CALL newPutForth(1031, 1, 'Music enriches the soul. That\'s not a cliche — it\'s an observation about what human beings have used music for across every culture and every era. It does something no other art form does in quite the same way.');
CALL newPutForth(1032, 1, 'Some albums are tied to specific moments — they capture the feeling of a year or a generation so completely that hearing them later transports you. That connection between music and memory is real and worth taking seriously.');
CALL newPutForth(1033, 1, 'One album encapsulates the 90s better than any other. The argument is worth having, because getting it right means identifying what the decade actually was — not just what was popular.');
CALL newPutForth(1046, 1, 'Automatic for the People is one of the most perfectly realized albums of the 90s. REM made a record about loss, mortality, and quiet grief that somehow became a mainstream hit. Its staying power is extraordinary.');
CALL newPutForth(1066, 1, 'Every decade has one album that captures it better than the rest. The aughts have a clear answer if you\'re willing to follow the logic to the right place.');
CALL newPutForth(1069, 1, 'Flogging Molly is named after an Irish pub they played so often they were figuratively beating it. The choice still deserves reflection — especially for a band with explicit Irish cultural ties.');
CALL newPutForth(1073, 1, 'Speakerboxxx/The Love Below is the album of the aughts. Outkast took double-album ambition and produced two completely different masterpieces on one release — Big Boi\'s tightly constructed funk and Andre 3000\'s genre-defying avant-pop.');
CALL newPutForth(1074, 1, 'Flogging Molly took their name from an Irish bar in Los Angeles where they played so frequently it felt like they were flogging the place. That\'s the origin — a bit of self-deprecating humor about overworking a venue.');
CALL newPutForth(1076, 1, '2001 was released in 1999, which disqualifies it from best-of-decade arguments about the 2000s regardless of its quality. Chronological integrity matters if decade rankings are going to mean anything at all.');
CALL newPutForth(1081, 1, '2001 by Dr. Dre peaked on the charts in December 1999. The release date question isn\'t ambiguous — it\'s not an aughts album. It\'s an excellent record that belongs in the 90s conversation, not the 2000s.');
CALL newPutForth(1082, 1, 'One album will stand above the rest as the defining statement of the 2020s decade — the question of which one is still being answered, but the argument is worth starting.');
CALL newPutForth(1083, 1, 'SAULT\'s Untitled (Rise) is the album of the 2020s so far. It\'s anonymous, politically alive, and made with a discipline and integrity that feels genuinely rare. The anonymity makes the music work harder.');
CALL newPutForth(1085, 1, 'Art is inseparable from its historical moment. The best work reflects something true about the conditions that produced it, and understanding those conditions is part of understanding the art.');
CALL newPutForth(1086, 1, 'Some films transcend entertainment and become cultural documents — you can\'t fully understand a decade without them. That\'s a small category, but it\'s real.');
CALL newPutForth(1088, 1, 'Old School is a legitimate comedy classic of the aughts — surprisingly well-constructed, genuinely funny, and more emotionally grounded than its premise suggests. Will Ferrell\'s Frank the Tank is one of the great comedic characters of the era.');
CALL newPutForth(1090, 1, 'Blink-182 is a seminal band whether you love them or hate them. They defined a moment, moved enormous amounts of music, and shaped the sound and aesthetic of an entire teenage generation. You don\'t get to dismiss that.');
CALL newPutForth(1092, 1, 'Machine Gun Kelly is making Blink-182 songs for a younger audience. The pop punk revival he\'s riding is commercially real, but the music is derivative. He found a formula that works and repeated it.');
CALL newPutForth(1094, 1, 'Machine Gun Kelly is unlistenable — not because of the genre, but because of the execution. There\'s no tension in the music, no edge, no surprise. It\'s a surface-level imitation of a form that required real angst to work.');
CALL newPutForth(1097, 1, 'Artists and their work should be judged in historical context. The standards, norms, and power structures of a given era shaped what was made and what was considered acceptable. That doesn\'t excuse everything, but it\'s a necessary lens.');
CALL newPutForth(1098, 1, 'Outdated or offensive content in art and media should be presented with context, not erased. Censorship hides history. Contextualization confronts it. Adults can engage with difficult material if we trust them with the full story.');
CALL newPutForth(1104, 1, 'Woody Allen\'s films are technically accomplished but overrated as art. The reputation is partly a product of critical gatekeeping and partly genuine craft — but his cultural stature far exceeds what the films have earned.');
CALL newPutForth(1105, 1, 'Woody Allen\'s films speak to a specific, narrow, upper-middle-class New York sensibility. That viewpoint has its place in cinema, but it was always a niche perspective that got treated as universal for longer than it should have.');
CALL newPutForth(1110, 1, 'Michael Jackson is a monster. The evidence — documentary testimony, corroborating details, the pattern of behavior — is overwhelming. The musical legacy doesn\'t change what he did.');
CALL newPutForth(1111, 1, 'Michael Jackson raped children. The documentary evidence in Leaving Neverland and the corroborating legal record make this the only reasonable conclusion. Acquittal in a criminal trial is not exoneration.');
CALL newPutForth(1113, 1, 'Michael Jackson was acquitted of molestation charges in 2005. That\'s a legal fact. It\'s also insufficient to settle the moral question, given what we know about criminal justice outcomes, settlements, and the power dynamics involved.');
CALL newPutForth(1115, 1, 'Courts get it wrong all the time — that\'s well documented. My obligations don\'t end at accepting a verdict I have reason to disbelieve. When the evidence outside the courtroom is this strong, the acquittal is a data point, not the final word.');
CALL newPutForth(1119, 1, 'I won\'t play, listen to, or promote Michael Jackson\'s music. That\'s a personal choice I\'ve made based on the weight of evidence about what he did. Others can disagree, but I can\'t pretend I don\'t know what I know.');
CALL newPutForth(1124, 1, 'Michael Jackson\'s legacy needs to be corrected. He\'s still treated as an icon — played on the radio, streamed, celebrated. The cultural accounting hasn\'t happened yet. It needs to.');
CALL newPutForth(1125, 1, 'Jackson\'s fame operates independently of his art. He\'s worshipped as a cultural hero in ways that actively harm survivors. As long as that\'s true, the appropriate response is to diminish that fame, not just detach it from the music.');
CALL newPutForth(1126, 1, 'The survivors of Michael Jackson\'s abuse are still alive and still being gaslit by fans who refuse to believe them. Every defense of Jackson, every refusal to engage with the evidence, is another way of siding with the abuser.');
CALL newPutForth(1130, 1, 'Michael Jackson is still celebrated as a hero. The reverence persists — in tributes, in streaming numbers, in the way his name is still invoked as a standard of greatness. That\'s the cultural reality that needs to change.');
CALL newPutForth(1131, 1, 'I use \'to a degree\' deliberately — the worship of Michael Jackson isn\'t universal, and there are gradations. But the trend is clear enough that examples are easy to find, and the problem is real enough to address.');
CALL newPutForth(1132, 1, 'Leaving Neverland is credible. The detail of the accounts, the corroboration, the pattern — I see no reason to doubt the major events described. The documentary should change how Michael Jackson is discussed.');
CALL newPutForth(1134, 1, 'Worshipping celebrities to any degree is unhealthy. It substitutes projection for genuine relationship, creates parasocial dependencies that aren\'t reciprocal, and produces exactly the conditions that protect people like Michael Jackson.');
CALL newPutForth(1215, 1, 'The MCU has flattened the individual stories of superheroes into franchise delivery mechanisms. Every film has to set up the next one, service the broader mythology, and answer to continuity. That\'s no way to tell a story.');
CALL newPutForth(1216, 1, 'Hollywood finds great independent work, acquires it, sands off its edges, and scales it up until it\'s palatable to the widest possible audience. What made the original thing interesting is usually what gets removed in the process.');
CALL newPutForth(1220, 1, 'Making your hero invincible except for one specific vulnerability is lazy writing. Kryptonite is a storytelling crutch. Good characters have real limitations — moral, psychological, situational — not just a magic mineral that turns off their powers.');
CALL newPutForth(1221, 1, 'DC comics have historically underserved the human dimensions of their characters. Superman is a god who pretends to be human. Batman is more interesting, but even there the humanity is often secondary to the mythology.');
CALL newPutForth(1222, 1, 'Bruce Wayne will never stop carrying the guilt of his parents\' murder. That\'s not a flaw in his character — it\'s his character. It drives everything he does and makes him more interesting than any hero who\'s made peace with their origin.');
CALL newPutForth(1226, 1, 'The most compelling superheroes are the ones we can see ourselves in — flawed, burdened by their gifts, trying to do right in a world that complicates everything. Invincibility makes for bad storytelling and worse characters.');
CALL newPutForth(1227, 1, 'Meteor Man from 1993 was the first black superhero feature film. It\'s underremembered but historically significant — it preceded Blade and Black Panther by decades and deserves acknowledgment in the history of the genre.');
CALL newPutForth(1228, 1, 'Blade was the first major theatrical release based on a Marvel comic, and Wesley Snipes brought genuine menace and charisma to the role. Without Blade\'s commercial success, the modern MCU era might not have happened.');
CALL newPutForth(1583, 1, 'I don\'t listen to Michael Jackson anymore. Not because I never liked the music — some of it was genuinely great. But I can\'t separate what I know about him from what I\'m hearing. It\'s a choice I\'ve made and I\'m comfortable with it.');
CALL newPutForth(1584, 1, 'I won\'t get on the dance floor for Michael Jackson. When his songs come on, I sit down. Some of my friends think that\'s excessive. I think it\'s the minimum appropriate response to what he did.');




